{"name":"Cobblestone Path","hex":"#9e8779","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cobblestone","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A rounded stone from a river bed, fit for use as ballast in ships and for paving roads.","Cobblestones viewed as a building or paving material."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cobblestone"},{"term":"path","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A trail for the use of, or worn by, pedestrians.","A course taken."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To make a path in, or on (something), or for (someone).","To navigate through a file system directory tree (to a desired file or folder)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/path"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cobblestone Path","blocks":[{"text":"The Cobblestone Path is a historic pathway along the east side of the Bardstown Historic District of Bardstown, Kentucky, passing by eight acres of land. Once longer, due to various construction it now remains between Flaget Avenue and Broadway, directly across from the Bardstown Civil War Museum. Because it was always considered part of the city's street system, the Cobblestone Path is owned by the City of Bardstown, even though it now serves only pedestrian traffic."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Cobblestone_Path_2.JPG/330px-Cobblestone_Path_2.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cobblestone_Path"},{"title":"List of cobblestone streets","blocks":[{"text":"A cobbled street or cobblestone road, is a street or road paved with cobblestones."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Cleaning_the_cobbles_at_Church_of_Santo_Domingo_de_Guzm%C3%A1n_%288263583297%29.jpg/330px-Cleaning_the_cobbles_at_Church_of_Santo_Domingo_de_Guzm%C3%A1n_%288263583297%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_cobblestone_streets"},{"title":"Cobblestone","blocks":[{"text":"Cobblestone is a natural building material based on cobble-sized stones, and is used for pavement roads, streets, and buildings. Setts, also called Belgian blocks, are often referred to as \"cobbles\", although a sett is distinct from a cobblestone by being quarried and shaped into a regular form, while cobblestones are naturally occurring rounded forms less uniform in size."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Ancient_road_surface.jpg/330px-Ancient_road_surface.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cobblestone"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Cobblestone_Path_1.JPG/120px-Cobblestone_Path_1.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Cobblestone_Path_1.JPG/250px-Cobblestone_Path_1.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Cobblestone_Path_1.JPG/120px-Cobblestone_Path_1.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACobblestone_Path_1.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Cobblestone_Path_3.JPG/120px-Cobblestone_Path_3.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Cobblestone_Path_3.JPG/250px-Cobblestone_Path_3.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Cobblestone_Path_3.JPG/120px-Cobblestone_Path_3.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACobblestone_Path_3.JPG"},{"srcset":"htt